= Introduction
Creating a computer capable of playing games has been part of Computer Science
research for a long time. It is a benchmark of human intelligence and
an exploration of the boundaries of what is capable using
artificial decision making. This culminated in the defeat of reigning world-chess
champion, Garry Kasparov, at the computations of the IBM chess computer DeepBlue
in 1997. #cite
This spurred on further improvement on chess machines and research on a multitude of other games. 
Another popular game by the name of Go remained a problem to
scientists for 19 more years. While many approaches were tested, it was not until 2016 that a
high-ranking professional player was beaten by Google-DeepMind's artificial
intelligence (AI), AlphaGo, that used a vastly different approach from earlier
game computers and programs. It combined Deep Neural Network (DNN) training with
exploration guided by distributions output by the neural network. #cite


== Gap
A problem that is apparent with most AIs is the amount of computation that is
necessary to achieve good results. The literature is abundant with examples of
large networks and decision making algorithms that reasonably require too much
computing power. Therefore, it is necessary to improve efficiency of algorithms
by all possible means. Especially, algorithms that make use Monte Carlo Tree
Search (MCTS) seem to tend to execute a lot of unnecessary computation because it
executes the allowed number of simulations without consideration as to whether 
the return move probability distribution changes and such computation is necessary.


== Hypothesis
The central claim of this thesis is that efficiency of MCTS can be increased by
dynamically adjusting the amount of simulations based on the prior move
probabilties generated by the DNN while upholding parity in performance. The
development and implementation of such a heuristic are the goal of this thesis.



= Background knowledge
== Go
Go is a Chinese board game that can be traced back to 4,000 years ago.  It is
played by two-players using black and white stones. While usually played on a 19
#math.times 19-grid, other board sizes like 9 #math.times 9 or 13 #math.times 13
exist. 
The goal of the game is to enclose more territory, i.e., grid
intersections, than the opponent.

The players place stones in turn; the player with the black stones begins. The
group of free places around a single stone are called liberties, see @liberties. 
Stones of the same colour that are placed on neighbouring fields are considered
a group and share the liberties. A roup may be arbitrarily large. A
group of the opponent can be captured by filling in all liberties of the
group, see @capturing. While generally any stone may be placed anywhere on the
board, it may not be placed in places where the stone or its group dies because
of the stone being placed. However, such a stone may be placed if placing this
stone means capturing an opponent's group.
Importantly, a board position may not be immediately repeated. This means,
after a stone has been captured, and the capturing stone may not be recaptured
on the next move.
This is called Ko. #fill[Appendix]

