The game ends when both players pass consecutively.
Usually, players pass when all necessary moves are played and the territories
are firmly established. Afterwards, the territory is counted. For that, players
place back their prisoner stones into the opponent's territory. The remaining
free grid intersections within their territory are the points for the respective
player.
To account for black's advantage in going first and avoid draw, additional
5.5-7.5 points are awarded to white. This is called komi.

Komi can be adjusted to even out the differences in capability
between differently strong players. Lastly, komi can be combined with a handicap
that places black stones on predetermined spots on the board.

Traditionally, players, human or computational, are attributed ranks based on 
strength. The weakest players play at 30 kyu. You can improve until reaching 1 
kyu. From there, you can improve to 1 dan and theoretically up to 9 dan. Most 
top-level amateur players become 6 dan. Professional players are ranked 
separately with their own dan-scale. Nowadays, players are also evaluated using
the Elo-system.


= Technical Foundations

== Reinforcement Learning and Self-Play
Reinforcement Learning (RL) describes a Machine Learning paradigm in which an
agent is trained to maximize a reward signal in interaction with its
environment.
This makes use of two concepts called exploitation and exploration. To exploit
is to choose a path of action that is known to produce a good result. On the
other side, to explore is to choose a path of action that is not well-known and
thus, upon further examination, may produce a better result than already known
paths of action.
Self-play is a specialised framework within the field of RL. In self-play, an
algorithm plays a match in a game against an earlier iteration of itself. In
this case, the previous iteration is the environment and the reward signal is
the outcome of the match. Various selection strategies exist for the
comparison-iteration, e.g., a random iteration is chosen to play, or the
selection follows a "King-of-the-Hill"-approach, where the strongest iteration
remains as the benchmark and is to be beaten. This, more than the randomised
selection shows growth in performance of the agent.




== Deep Neural Networks

=== Artificial Neural Networks
Today, artificial neural networks (ANN) are one of the most used and researched
technologies. With their groundwork laid in 1958, new methods have been since
been developed for a large number of purposes, not the least of which is playing
games and improving beyond human capabilities.

Generally, ANNs receive an input of defined but arbitrary format. The signal of 
this input are propagated through the network to the output layer. One layer is
made up of multiple neurons, each with their own weights per input, plus a bias
individual to each neuron. In the 
context to RL, the ANN's output is evaluated for its reward signal in response 
to the environment. From this, an error signal is generated and passed backwards 
through the network. Each layer is updated dependent on the succeeding layer's 
error signal.


=== Residual Neural Networks
In the course of research and development of ANNs, they have grown exponentially
deeper. Beginning with a single perceptron, which were then used in a single 
layers, now, there are networks using neurons with more than 100 layers and a 
large number of perceptrons per layer. Users of ANNs of this size noticed the 
"Vanishing Gradient" problem. In the process of backpropagation, the 
derivatives' chain rule is employed. For that, multiple small decimal numbers
are repeatedly multiplied. This produces even smaller decimal numbers
The "Vanishing Gradient" describes the phenomenon that the error signal
that early layers of the networks receive is too small to effectively change the
weights and biases of those layers. This stops learning from having any effect.


