To solve this problem, residual neural networks were introduced in Deep Learning
(DL) problems. Residual neural networks (ResNet) make use of  "skipping
connections." In one of such connections, a residual layer projects its output to 
another
layer further down the stack of layers, skipping the layers inbetween. The
residual output is added to the output of the layer, to which it is is
projected.
This largely
fixes the Vanishing Gradient since the output of the layer is passed further
into the stack of layers. 

A negative point in this setup is that the intermediate layers may be found to
be superfluous and converge to $0$ during training.





== Monte Carlo Tree Search
MCTS is a heuristic decision making algorithm. Through exploration of subsequent
states from a root state, MCTS finds a state that is most likely to maximise a
metric that can be changed per use-case. 
During the search, a tree is built from the original state as
root node R. There are four stages to MCTS:
+ Selection: From a parent node P, choose a child node according to a selection
  method.
  Repeat until choosing a leaf node C.
+ Expansion: If C is not a terminal state, i.e., there is a possible following
  state, create new child nodes. Choose a child state.
+ Evaluation: A state may be evaluated by choosing random subsequent states
  until a terminal state which has a definitive value. Another method is an
  evaluation function applied to the state.
+ Backpropagation: Update the probabilities in the path from C to R using the
  result from the rollout.
Through rigorous exploration of the tree by simulations, i.e., paths from root
to terminal state, the child states most likely to maximise the evaluation
method converge to higher values in the selection method.

The selection method most commonly used in the selection phase is the "Upper
Confidence Bound 1 applied to Trees" (UCT). It is used to find a balance
between exploitation of well-simulated moves and exploration of less-simulated
moves in MCTS.
In the selection stage, the node with the highest value according to the
expression
$w_i/n_i + c sqrt(ln N_i / n_i)$
is selected, where 
- $w_i$ is the the wins for prospective C after the $i$-th move,
- $n_i$ is the visits for prospective C after the $i$-th node,
- $N_i$ is the visits for P after the $i$-th move, and
- $c$ is the exploration parameter.

== BayesElo
While simple win rates provide a snapshot of performance, they are sensitive to the specific pairings of a tournament. The BayesElo algorithm is a Bayesian refinement of the classic Elo rating system, specifically designed to handle the "Komi" (first-move advantage) and the possibility of draws—though draws are virtually non-existent in Go due to the fractional 7.5 point Komi.
The Mathematical Framework

BayesElo assumes that the performance of a model i in a given game is a random variable following a logistic distribution centered at its true rating Ri​. The probability P of model A defeating model B is expressed as:
$ P(A>B)=1+10^((R_B−R_A)/400) $

In this system, a rating difference of 200 points signifies that the stronger model has a 75% expected win rate, while a 400-point lead indicates a 90% win rate.


